Write this client's first gym week. The daily tracker reads the text, so the shape below is required.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[CLIENT DETAILS]

HOW TO WRITE IT:

1. Open with 2 to 4 sentences to the client by first name. Mention their goal and one real detail from their answers. Do not mention a coaching week number. NEVER write "Welcome to week", "week N", or a cross-day pointer.

2. Label every day "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. After the colon, use a plain focus: Push, Pull, Legs, Upper, Lower, Chest and arms, Back and arms, or Rest. Example: "Day 1 (Monday): Push". Do not put Power, Hypertrophy, or Density in the day title.

3. Pick one split and keep it. Use upper/lower, or push/pull/legs when they train 3 or 6 to 7 days. Training days per week is a hard cap. Leftover days are Rest. One region per day. Never a full-body day.
   A Push or Chest day is presses, dips, raises, and triceps only. No row, pulldown, or pull-up.
   A Pull or Back day is rows, pulldowns, pull-ups, and biceps curls only. No bench press, incline press, shoulder press, dip, push-up, or fly.
   An Upper day may include a press and a row. It has no squat, leg press, lunge, hinge, or calf raise.
   A Lower or Legs day is a squat or leg press, a Romanian deadlift or hip hinge, and calves. No press, row, pulldown, curl, or triceps movement.
   A core finisher may sit at the end of Main Workout on any day. Do not put the same working exercise on two calendar days in a row.

4. Use only common exercises a person can search on YouTube. Allowed names: Barbell Bench Press, Dumbbell Bench Press, Incline Bench Press, Shoulder Press, Push Up, Dip, Lat Pulldown, Seated Row, Dumbbell Row, Barbell Row, Pull Up, Chin Up, Squat, Goblet Squat, Leg Press, Romanian Deadlift, Deadlift, Lunge, Split Squat, Leg Curl, Leg Extension, Hip Thrust, Glute Bridge, Calf Raise, Biceps Curl, Triceps Pushdown, Lateral Raise, Face Pull, Plank, Crunch. Do not invent a move. Do not write Pallof press, prone Y raise, tempo, pause, or deficit in the exercise name. If equipment or an injury blocks a lift, swap it for another name on this list.

5. Under every training day, put these labels on their own lines, in order: Warm-up: then Main Workout: then Post-Workout:. Warm-up is an easy walk or bike and joint circles only. Do not put a named lift in the warm-up, and never repeat an exercise that is also in Main Workout. Each exercise name once per day. Dumbbell Bench Press and Barbell Bench Press are the same bench press, so write only one. Write only one row. Main Workout is the working lifts, about 5 to 7 of them. Post-Workout is stretches only. Do not add a shared warmup after the week. The daily tracker cannot resolve cross-day references. NEVER write "same as Day 1", "repeat Monday", or "as above". If two days share a session, rewrite the complete exercise list under both headers.

6. Write each working exercise as "Exercise: N sets x M reps. One short sentence in everyday words." Example: "Barbell Bench Press: 3 sets x 8 reps. Lie on the bench, lower the bar to your chest, then press it back up." Use a plain letter x. Never 5 or more working sets. Set counts follow the TRAINING VOLUME rules. Beginners use 3 working sets. Intermediate and advanced use 4 on the main lifts and 3 on the smaller ones. Beginners use 8 to 15 reps. Intermediate use 6 to 12. On the main lifts, write "stop when you could still do 2 more clean reps." Do not write RIR, RDL, hypertrophy, mesocycle, compounds, isolation, delts, or posterior chain.

7. Use only gym stations listed in Hard Constraints, plus bodyweight. Do not assume a machine they do not have. Weave one line for a daily step target. Add a short sleep note of 7.5 to 9 hours. Do not add a Cardio, Conditioning, or Supplements header. Do not write calorie targets in the workout. Close with two encouraging sentences. Never use a hyphen, en dash, or em dash. Write "6 to 8" not "6-8".

8. If anything here conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.
